WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.112 --> 00:00:03.612
(bright percussive music)

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.170
- An engineer in our society,
they don't look like me,

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.368
but I'm here to break that mold.

00:00:10.368 --> 00:00:13.868
(bright percussive music)

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.340
My name is Dajae “Moe” Williams,

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.410
and it's kind of hard to explain, but I ...

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:22.400
- [Gillian Jacobs] Don't worry, Dajae.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.850
Dajae is a manufacturing engineer

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.000
at NASA's Jet Propulsion Lab,
and she's also a musician.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:29.880
- Well said.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.930
I literally couldn't have
said it better myself.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:32.960
- [Gillian Jacobs] Thank you, Dajae.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.020
Lets get back to where it all started.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.740
Dajae's love of science all began

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.800
when she was growing up in St. Louis.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.570
- I grew up on the south
side of St. Louis, Missouri,

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.000
in a single-parent household with my mom.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.070
I got into science because of a program

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.700
that my mom kicked down
doors to get me into.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.390
They exposed us to
different science topics,

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.420
such as robotics, chemistry, you name it.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.330
- [Gillian Jacobs] In college,

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.450
Dajae studied engineering management.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.740
While there, she faced more

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.640
than just challenging schoolwork.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.120
- A lot of times, in class,
I was the only female.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.420
I was the only Black female.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.458
Sometimes people didn't wanna sit by you.

00:01:08.458 --> 00:01:10.890
Sometimes people didn't wanna help you.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.580
You know, I would see a lot of times

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.650
that people would have
their, I'm trying not to cry.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.930
People would have their
little groups that they would

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.330
study with, and I would
have to, like, infiltrate.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.330
Like, how can I bond with
that group so that I can

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:28.420
get the same resources
that my people don't have?

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:29.700
But I figured it out.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.240
- [Gillian Jacobs] Of
course she figured it out.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.963
Which brings us to NASA.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.730
Dajae is part of a team
building rocket ships.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.060
They take an engineer's
idea and figure out

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.760
how to make it a reality,
which is pretty freakin' cool.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.100
Now, remember a minute
ago when we mentioned

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.050
that Dajae was also a musician?

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.660
She wrote a song about science

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.390
to the tune of Soulja Boy's “Crank That.”

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.866
- I was just writing it
out, like, “Yo, this is hot!”

00:01:54.866 --> 00:01:56.247
♪ X equals in this oh ♪

00:01:56.247 --> 00:01:57.927
♪ Negative b, less or more ♪

00:01:57.927 --> 00:02:00.759
♪ The square root of b squared
minus four ac fo' sho' ♪

00:02:00.759 --> 00:02:03.079
♪ Now find the root over two a ♪

00:02:03.079 --> 00:02:04.807
♪ Root over two a ♪

00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:06.937
♪ Root over two a ♪

00:02:06.937 --> 00:02:08.687
♪ Ey ♪

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.689
- [Gillian Jacobs] Dajae
realized that through music,

00:02:11.689 --> 00:02:14.330
you could open up a
whole new audience's eyes

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.140
to the world of science,
something she wished

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.750
she had when she was young.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.780
- It's not just writing
a song, and that's it.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.440
Like, I actually take it a
step further and am a mentor

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.020
to a lot of students who either
are interested in science

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.840
or are already in college and just need

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.130
that extra push to continue and finish.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.600
I wanna show through my life that

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.280
you can be an engineer,
you can be a musician.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.239
Yo, the sky is the limit.

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:41.739
(bright percussive music)

00:02:44.889 --> 00:02:48.556
(vibrato high-pitched note)

